@page "/privacy"
@attribute [AllowAnonymous]
@layout PublicLayout

<PageTitle>Privacy Approach | HIP Human Identity Protocol</PageTitle>
<HeadContent>
    <meta name="description" content="Read HIP's privacy-first approach to website scanning, client telemetry, identifiers, retention and public trust evidence." />
</HeadContent>

<main class="hip-public-page">
    <section class="hip-page-hero hip-grid-bg">
        <div class="hip-container">
            <p class="hip-eyebrow">Privacy approach</p>
            <h1>Collect less. Explain <span>what remains.</span></h1>
            <p class="hip-lead">HIP is designed to evaluate public trust and risk evidence without collecting passwords, form values, private messages, cookies, browsing history or unrelated raw page content.</p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="hip-section">
        <div class="hip-container">
            <div class="hip-capability-grid">
                <article><span>Minimize</span><h3>Use the smallest useful signal</h3><p>HIP prefers domains, URL hashes, signal counts, scores, reasons, provider names and timestamps over raw private content.</p></article>
                <article><span>Separate</span><h3>Distinguish evidence types</h3><p>Client observations remain separate from authoritative scans until verified evidence supports a public result.</p></article>
                <article><span>Protect</span><h3>Keep secrets out</h3><p>Passwords, tokens, cookies, private messages, sensitive identifiers and private evidence do not belong in public APIs, logs or test fixtures.</p></article>
            </div>
            <div class="hip-notice" style="margin-top: 42px;"><strong>Public overview</strong><p>This page summarizes HIP's engineering privacy defaults. Deployment-specific notices and legally required disclosures must remain accurate for the operator and jurisdiction where HIP is offered.</p></div>
        </div>
    </section>
</main>
